# clasp holds the Apps Script project id, which is deployment-scoped. Commit the example
# (.clasp.json.example) so the workflow is documented, never the real id.
#
# Both folders need their own line. Naming only one left the request endpoint's script id
# tracked, which means a single `git add .` would have committed a live deployment id --
# the exact thing this rule exists to prevent. A glob is safer than a second literal,
# because adding a third endpoint folder should not require remembering to extend a list.
docs/apps-script/.clasp.json
docs/requests-script/.clasp.json
**/.clasp.json
!**/.clasp.json.example

# Demo video build output. Captures, generated MP4/GIF and the contact sheets are
# large binaries that are re-runnable from tools/, not source: `node tools/record_demo.mjs`
# plus `python tools/build_demo.py` regenerates all of it. Committing them would put
# tens of megabytes of video into the history of a public repository, where they cannot
# be replaced once pushed.
build/
